<h2>Why Commercial Claims in Bexar County Get Complicated</h2>
<p>Bexar County gets most of its hail from March through May, and NOAA has logged hundreds of severe thunderstorm wind reports in the county, including the May 27, 1997 downburst estimated at more than 100 mph across southwest San Antonio. Large, flat, and low-slope commercial roofs present a lot of surface area to hail and wind. Damage to membranes, metal panels, rooftop HVAC units, and flashing can be hard to see from the ground and easy to understate.</p>
<p>Multi-building properties add more layers. An apartment community or HOA may have dozens of roofs, each with slightly different damage, and each building may be listed separately on the policy schedule. Freeze events like Winter Storm Uri in February 2021 and the ice storms of recent winters can cause burst sprinkler and supply lines that damage many units at once. And because commercial repairs generally require permits through the City of San Antonio Development Services Department or a suburban permit office, code requirements can affect scope and cost.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Claim-Preparation.webp" alt="Business owner reviewing financial charts and a calculator at a desk" width="640" height="427" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Business income claims depend on solid financial records as well as building damage documentation.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Business Income and Extra Expense</h2>
<p>For many businesses, the largest part of a loss is not physical damage but lost income. Business income coverage, when included, may pay lost net income and continuing normal operating expenses during the "period of restoration" after covered physical damage. Extra expense coverage may pay reasonable costs to avoid or reduce a shutdown, such as temporary space or expedited repairs. For rental properties, loss of rents coverage serves a similar purpose.</p>
<p>These coverages typically require:</p>
<ul>
<li>Profit and loss statements and tax returns from prior periods</li>
<li>Payroll records and a list of continuing expenses</li>
<li>Records showing the dates operations were affected</li>
<li>Invoices for extra expenses and temporary measures</li>
<li>Rent rolls and lease information for rental properties</li>
</ul>
<p>Waiting periods, limits, and the definition of the period of restoration vary by policy, so the calculation needs to follow your policy language. A forensic accountant may be appropriate on larger business income claims.</p>

<h2>Apartment, Multifamily, and HOA Claims</h2>
<p>Apartment communities and associations face a particular set of challenges. A single hailstorm can damage every roof on a property, but not always to the same degree, and an insurer may approve some buildings while denying others. Each building should be inspected and documented on its own, with photos tied to building numbers and elevations, so nothing is lost when the estimate is written.</p>
<p>For associations, the first question is often who insures what. The governing documents and the master policy define which parts of the structure the association covers and which fall to unit owners. Roofs, exterior walls, and common areas typically sit with the association, while interior finishes may belong to owners, but the details vary from one community to the next. Boards also need to keep owners informed, follow their own procedures for approving contracts, and plan how repairs will be scheduled across occupied buildings. For rental properties, tracking which units were uninhabitable and for how long supports any loss of rents claim.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Our Commercial Claim Process</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Policy and schedule review.</strong> We review the declarations, building schedule, endorsements, deductibles, coinsurance provisions, and any cosmetic damage or roof exclusions.</li>
<li><strong>Site inspection.</strong> We inspect roofs, building envelopes, mechanical equipment, and interiors, documenting each building and unit separately where needed.</li>
<li><strong>Estimating.</strong> We prepare detailed Xactimate estimates and, where appropriate, coordinate with roofing, engineering, or other specialists for complex systems.</li>
<li><strong>Business income support.</strong> We help organize the financial documentation the insurer will request.</li>
<li><strong>Claim presentation and follow-up.</strong> We submit the claim package, respond to requests, attend re-inspections, and track the insurer's timelines under the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims statute.</li>
<li><strong>Supplements and disputes.</strong> We document hidden damage found during repairs and, where the dispute is over the amount of loss, discuss whether <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> may fit.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Common Commercial Policy Provisions to Watch</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Coinsurance.</strong> If the building was insured for less than the required percentage of its value, the payment may be reduced.</li>
<li><strong>Per-building or per-occurrence deductibles.</strong> Percentage wind/hail deductibles can apply to each building's value.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage exclusions.</strong> Some policies exclude dents to metal roofing or siding that do not affect function.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law.</strong> Code upgrade costs are only covered to the extent this coverage applies. Our article on <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-antonio/">building codes and insurance repairs in San Antonio</a> explains more.</li>
<li><strong>Protective safeguards and vacancy.</strong> Conditions about sprinklers, alarms, or vacancy can affect coverage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li>Report the loss promptly to the insurer and, for associations, to the board and management company.</li>
<li>Take reasonable steps to protect the property, such as emergency roof repairs and water extraction, and keep invoices.</li>
<li>Preserve damaged materials and equipment until documented.</li>
<li>Keep financial records organized for any business income claim.</li>
<li>Coordinate with tenants about their own insurance obligations under their leases.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Working With a San Antonio Public Adjuster on a Commercial Loss</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster serves business and property owners across San Antonio, Bexar County, and surrounding communities such as <a href="/service-areas/new-braunfels/">New Braunfels</a>, Schertz, and Universal City. Joseph Dittman's experience includes commercial, storm, and catastrophe losses, along with carrier-side adjusting and appraisal work. We cannot promise any particular outcome; coverage depends on the policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. For a first look at your claim, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
